<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kroměříž</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kremsier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kroměříž, in German Kremsier, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, long the summer seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, whose palace and gardens are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. From November 1848 to March 1849 the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> sat there and drafted a liberal constitution, but it was dissolved by the government before the draft could take effect.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kromeriz.jpg" alt="Kroměříž" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Frank from Wien (Vienna), AT · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krom%C4%9B%C5%99%C3%AD%C5%BE._Ausblick_vom_Schlossturm_(25007770188).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
